        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        },
        {
          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5
    ,
      "why": "unrolled_k trades a little area (k parallel shift/add stages) for higher throughput: results/cycle = k, so throughput = Fmax*k/ceil(N/k). For the same N and W it can meet >=1 MSPS at a lower Fmax (hence potentially lower area via shorter critical path per stage) than iterative, or meet it at the same Fmax with more margin. Searching k=2..4 maps the area-vs-throughput trade-off that iterative alone cannot express, so it covers the rest of the feasible front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Only iterative and unrolled_k are available to me, and both are the low-area, multi-cycle families (pipelined/pipelined_m, which would dominate on throughput, are assigned to other fleet members). The spec is area-minimisation subject to throughput >= 1 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Accuracy is easy: N=10-16 with W=12-16 gives accuracy_bits well above 10, so I keep n_iter and data_width in a tight band that guarantees the error constraint while leaving room for the area trade-off (larger W/N costs area). The binding constraint is throughput: iterative gives Fmax/(N+3) and unrolled_k gives Fmax*k/ceil(N/k), so I let Optuna explore the W/N/k space where Fmax is high enough to clear 1 MSPS. I split the budget evenly between the two families because iterative likely owns the absolute min-area point while unrolled_k can reach the same throughput at a lower required Fmax (shorter per-stage path), potentially finding a smaller-area point at the throughput boundary. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small (0..2 / 0..1) since extra guard bits add area without helping the 2^-10 error target, and both rounding modes are kept so the model can pick the cheaper one. This focuses the 100 evals on the region that actually defines the Pareto front for this spec."
}

